r' \\g<tz>', date_str)\n    ...\n```\n\nHonestly, the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n\n    date_str = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', date_str)\n    date_str = date_str.strip()\n    date_str = re.sub(r'(?i)[,\\s]+(?P<tz>[A-Z]{1,4})', r' \\g<tz>', date_str)\n\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?P<tz>[+-]\\d\\d?):?(?P<minutes>\\d\\d)?(?P<seconds>\\d\\d)?$', date_str)\n    if m:\n        timezone = datetime.timedelta(\n            hours=int(m.group('tz')),\n            minutes=int(m.group('minutes') or 0),\n            seconds=int(m.group('seconds') or 0))\n        date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n    else:\n        timezone = datetime.timedelta()\n\n    m = re.search(r'(?i)([ap])\\.?m\\.?(?:[+-]|$)', date_str)\n    if m:\n        ...\n\n    upper_dates = re.sub(...)\n    ...\n    for regex in (\n            r'(?P<year>\\d{4})-(?P<month>\\d{1,2})-(?P<day>\\d{1,2})(?:[ T](?P<hour>\\d{1,2}):(?P<minute>\\d{1,2}):(?P<second>\\d{1,2})(?:\\.(?P<microsecond>\\d+))?)?',\n            ...\n    ):\n        m = re.match(regex, date_str)\n        if m:\n            ...\n            return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple()) - timezone.total_seconds()\n```\n\nSomething like that. With 'Z' suffix... Hmm, 'Z' \u2014 there's handling: `date_str = date_str.replace('Z', ' +0000')`? I'm not 100% sure. Let me check via get_info('unified_timestamp').\n\nTo be safe, maybe use dates without 'Z': '2019-01-01T10:00:00' or '2019-01-01 10:00:00'. If timezone is not detected, timezone=0 and timegm is used \u2192 UTC interpretation. So '2019-01-01T10:00:00' \u2192 1546336800. And with 'Z'... if 'Z' isn't stripped, the regex `(?P<year>\\d{4})-...` with re.match would still match the prefix (re.match anchors at start, not end), leaving 'Z' unmatched \u2014 but then... hmm, if the regex matches a prefix, the code might still parse it. Actually in youtube-dl's unified_timestamp, I believe there's explicit 'Z' handling. Let me just check with the tool.\n\nAlternatively, use a format that's unambiguous and well-tested: '2019-01-01 10:00:00 UTC'? Hmm, 'UTC' handling exists. Or just use ISO without TZ. I'll check the tool output.\n\nLet me also double check `parse_duration('5 min')`:\n\n```python\ndef parse_duration(s):\n    ...\n    units_re = r'(?P<unit>y|year|month|w|week|d|day|h|hour|m|min|minute|s|sec|second)'\n    ...\n```\n\n'5 min' \u2192 5 minutes \u2192 300. '1 hour' \u2192 3600. '6 min' \u2192 360. '7 min' \u2192 420. Good.\n\n`clean_html('<b>bold desc</b>')` \u2192 'bold desc'. `clean_html('<p>Fallback course description</p>')` \u2192 'Fallback course description'. `clean_html('<i>text desc</i>')` \u2192 'text desc'. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: `_search_regex` in the course path: `r'window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;'`. Our webpage has `window.module = {...};` \u2014 the regex `({.+?})` non-greedy... The JSON contains nested braces; `.+?` non-greedy up to `}` followed by `\\s*;`. The JSON object ends with `}` then `;`. But there are inner `}` characters followed by... inner `}` are followed by `,` or `]` etc., not `;`. The last `}` before `;` is the object close. But wait: `.+?` with re.search \u2014 it finds the earliest start. `window.module = ` then `({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 the first `{` after `=`, then minimal chars until `}\\s*;`. Inner `}`s: e.g. `\"items\": [ ... {\"type\": ...} ... ]` \u2014 an inner `}` followed by `,` \u2014 not `;`. The final `}` of module followed by `;` \u2014 matches. But careful: is there any `}` followed by `;` inside? In my JSON, no `;` inside. Good.\n\nBut hmm, `.+?` doesn't match newlines by default! `.` doesn't match `\\n`. My webpage has the JSON spanning multiple lines with newlines. `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 after `=`, `\\s*` matches the newline, then `({.+?})` starts at `{`, and `.+?` can't cross newlines. So the JSON must be on a single line! Important. Same for `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\