 `hasattr(ie, '_download_webpage')` is True (class method). It records the old value via `getattr`... hmm, actually monkeypatch.setattr does:\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    ...\n    self._setitem.append((target, name, oldval))  # actually it stores (target, name) and value\n```\n\nThen on undo, if oldval is notset, it deletes the attribute; otherwise sets it back. Since `getattr(ie, '_download_webpage')` returns the bound method, on undo it will set `ie._download_webpage = <bound method>` as an instance attribute \u2014 which effectively shadows the class method with the same bound method. That's fine functionally (the bound method is equivalent), though it leaves an instance attribute... but monkeypatch undoes it by setting it back, which leaves the instance attribute set to the bound method. Hmm, that's a slight pollution of the instance, but the instance is local to the test anyway, so no global state pollution. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, patch the class: `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_download_webpage', fake)`. That's cleaner in some ways. But then `fake` receives `self` as first arg. Let me patch the class with functions taking self:\n\n```python\ndef fake_download_webpage(self, url, video_id, **kwargs):\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, but with multiple IE instances it doesn't matter. I'll patch the instance for clarity, or the class. Either is fine. Let me patch the class to be safe with bound-method semantics:\n\nActually simpler: create the IE instance, then use `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download)` where fake_download takes (url, video_id). Since we set an instance attribute, calls to `self._download_webpage(...)` will find the instance attribute first. That works.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` with raisin