= date_str.replace('Z', '+0000')`? Hmm no... Actually I recall in `unified_timestamp`:\n\n```python\n    date_str = re.sub(r'(?i)\\s*(?:CEST|CET|EST|EDT|GMT|UTC|Z)$', '', date_str)\n```\n\nNot sure. The safest: check the tool. If unclear, use '2019-01-01 10:00:00' (space separator, no TZ) \u2014 that definitely parses via the first regex with `[ T]` separator, timezone=0, timegm \u2192 1546336800.\n\nHmm wait, but actually with no timezone info, does unified_timestamp treat it as UTC? The final return is `calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple()) - timezone.total_seconds()` \u2014 timegm treats dt as UTC. So yes, naive \u2192 UTC. So '2019-01-01 10:00:00' \u2192 1546336800. \n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's also a part where unified_timestamp tries `datetime.datetime.strptime` with various formats... The core logic I remember:\n\n```python\n    for regex in (...):\n        m = re.match(regex, date_str)\n        if m:\n            ...\n            return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple()) - timezone.total_seconds()\n\n    # Ask for the timezone only after the date is parsed?\n    ...\n    dt = ...  # strptime attempts\n    if dt:\n        return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())\n```\n\nWhatever \u2014 '2019-01-01 10:00:00' \u2192 1546336800 is safe. Let me use that format to be safe, unless the tool tells me 'Z' works. Actually, let me test both formats' expectations: I'll use '2019-01-01 10:00:00' style. Hmm, but real-world data would have ISO... doesn't matter for the test.\n\nActually wait, I want to double-check one thing about unified_timestamp: there's special handling where if the string contains a timezone name like 'UTC' it adjusts. With plain '2019-01-01 10:00:00', no tz \u2192 1546336800. I'm confident.\n\nLet me now also double check `parse_duration('5 min')` more carefully:\n\n```python\ndef parse_duration(s):\n    if not isinstance(s, compat_basestring):\n        return None\n\n    s = s.strip()\n\n    # https://www.w3.org/TR/NOTE-datetime\n    m = re.match(\n        r'